    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hussein-dey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussein-dey.jpg" alt="Hussein Dey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hussein-dey">Hussein Dey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dey_Hussein_Pacha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">German inscription, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> began as a punitive expedition. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hussein-dey">Hussein Dey</a> of the Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algiers">Algiers</a> struck the French consul with a fly-whisk in 1827 and a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naval-blockade">blockade</a> failed to bring him to terms, the government of Charles X, hoping for prestige before elections, sent an army of more than 30,000 men under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-de-bourmont">Louis de Bourmont</a>. It landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sidi-fredj">Sidi Ferruch</a> on 14 June 1830, and Algiers capitulated on 5 July; within weeks the July Revolution had swept Charles X from power. The July Monarchy hesitated over what to do with the conquest but stayed, and in 1834 it appointed a governor-general of the French possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a>. Resistance came from two directions: in the east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmed-bey-of-constantine">Ahmed Bey of Constantine</a>, whose city fell in 1837 after a failed first assault in 1836, and in the west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir-abdelkader">Abdelkader</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-desmichels">Treaty of Desmichels</a> in 1834 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-tafna">Treaty of Tafna</a> in 1837 before war resumed in 1839. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thomas-robert-bugeaud">Thomas-Robert Bugeaud</a>, governor-general from 1840, mobile columns destroyed crops, herds and villages until Abdelkader surrendered in December 1847. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kabylia">Kabylia</a> was subdued only in 1857, and the Mokrani revolt of 1871 was the last great rural uprising of the conquest era.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hassi-messaoud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hassi-messaoud.jpg" alt="Hassi Messaoud" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hassi-messaoud">Hassi Messaoud</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20070227_OUARGLA_Camel_Race.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Naoll, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colonial society was built on the transfer of land and on legal inequality. The state seized religious endowments, former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beylik">beylik</a> lands and the property of rebels, above all after the Mokrani revolt, and the Warnier law of 1873 opened collectively held tribal land to sale. European settlers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malta">Malta</a>; a law of 1889 made the children of foreign Europeans born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> French, and by 1954 there were about a million Europeans, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pieds-noirs">pieds-noirs</a>, beside some eight to nine million Muslim Algerians. Algerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a>, citizens since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cremieux-decree">Crémieux Decree</a> of 1870, formed a third community. War, the famine of 1866–1868 and epidemics caused heavy loss of life, and the Muslim population is generally thought to have fallen sharply in the decades after 1830 before recovering towards the end of the century. Settler farming made Algeria one of the world’s largest wine exporters, especially after phylloxera ravaged French vineyards from the 1870s, alongside cereals, citrus fruit, tobacco and esparto grass, while railways and ports tied the coast to the metropole. Most Algerians remained poor peasants, sharecroppers or labourers, and growing numbers migrated to France for work from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a> onwards. Oil was discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hassi-messaoud">Hassi Messaoud</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> in 1956, just as the colony entered its final crisis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isly"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-isly.jpg" alt="Battle of Isly" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isly">Battle of Isly</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vernet_-_Bataille_d%27Isly_-_1846.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> was the training ground and home base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armee-d-afrique">Armée d’Afrique</a>, which recruited both settlers and Algerians. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-foreign-legion">French Foreign Legion</a> was founded in 1831 for service there and later made its headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sidi-bel-abbes">Sidi Bel Abbès</a>, and the colony also produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zouaves">Zouaves</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tirailleurs">Tirailleurs</a> algériens, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spahis">Spahis</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chasseurs-d-afrique">Chasseurs d’Afrique</a>. The conquest was among the most brutal colonial wars of the century: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thomas-robert-bugeaud">Bugeaud</a>’s columns relied on the <em>razzia</em>, the seizure of livestock and grain, and in 1845 troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aimable-pelissier">Aimable Pélissier</a> asphyxiated hundreds of people sheltering in caves in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dahra">Dahra</a>. In 1844 Bugeaud defeated a Moroccan army supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir-abdelkader">Abdelkader</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isly">Battle of Isly</a>. Algerian soldiers fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and both world wars. In November 1942 Allied forces landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algiers">Algiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oran">Oran</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#operation-torch">Operation Torch</a>, and in 1943 Algiers became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a>, headed jointly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a> until de Gaulle took sole control; Algerian units then fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and in the liberation of France. The war of independence from 1954 eventually tied down some 400,000 or more French troops, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#conscription">conscripts</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-liberation-front-algeria">FLN</a>’s National Liberation Army, and saw the fortified and electrified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morice-line">Morice Line</a> built along the Tunisian border.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#statute-of-algeria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/statute-of-algeria.jpg" alt="Statute of Algeria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#statute-of-algeria">Statute of Algeria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_French_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans-Ulrich Herzog, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> was governed as an extension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> rather than as an ordinary colony. In 1848 the Second Republic divided the settled north into three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#departement">departments</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algiers">Algiers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oran">Oran</a> and Constantine, while military territories run through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arab-bureaux">Arab Bureaux</a> administered much of the interior; the Saharan south became the separate Southern Territories in 1902. A governor-general in Algiers headed the administration, and between 1881 and 1896 many services were attached directly to ministries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a>. From 1898 elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#financial-delegations">Financial Delegations</a>, dominated by settlers, voted the colonial budget, and Algeria received financial autonomy in 1900. Settlers elected deputies and senators to the French parliament and ran the self-governing communes, while most Algerians lived in mixed communes under appointed administrators. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> spoke of an “Arab kingdom”, but his <em>sénatus-consulte</em> of 1865 made Muslims French subjects who could become citizens only by renouncing Islamic personal status, which very few did. The <em>Code de l’<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indigenat">indigénat</a></em>, systematised in 1881, allowed administrators to punish Muslims summarily for offences that did not apply to Europeans. Reforms such as the Jonnart law of 1919 and the abortive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#blum-viollette-proposal">Blum–Viollette proposal</a> of 1936 changed little, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#statute-of-algeria">Statute of Algeria</a> of 1947 created an assembly elected by two unequal colleges, whose elections in 1948 were widely rigged.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#blida"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blida.jpg" alt="Blida" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#blida">Blida</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chrea_blida_snow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BullyanDZ, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>’s most important overseas possession and the only one settled on a large scale, and its fate shaped modern France as much as modern Algeria. The colony fixed the frontiers of the Algerian state, extending them deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a>, and left French as a language of administration, education and literature alongside Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berber-languages">Tamazight</a>. Colonial rule also produced the nationalism that ended it: the reformist Association of Algerian Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulama">Ulama</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abdelhamid-ben-badis">Abdelhamid Ben Badis</a> in 1931, the independence movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#messali-hadj">Messali Hadj</a> and the liberal nationalism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a>. The war of independence became an inspiration for anti-colonial movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a> and beyond; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frantz-fanon">Frantz Fanon</a>, who worked as a psychiatrist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#blida">Blida</a>, theorised it in <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#the-wretched-of-the-earth">The Wretched of the Earth</a></em> (1961), and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gillo-pontecorvo">Gillo Pontecorvo</a>’s film <em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-algiers">Battle of Algiers</a></em> (1966) made it known worldwide. In France the war brought down the Fourth Republic and produced the Fifth, and the army’s use of torture became a lasting subject of political and historical controversy. Writers born in the colony, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albert-camus">Albert Camus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kateb-yacine">Kateb Yacine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mohammed-dib">Mohammed Dib</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assia-djebar">Assia Djebar</a>, and the large Algerian, pied-noir and harki communities in France keep its memory alive and contested.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nationalist pressure grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a>. On 8 May 1945 demonstrations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#setif">Sétif</a> turned into riots in which about a hundred Europeans were killed, and the army, air force and settler militias answered with reprisals around Sétif and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guelma">Guelma</a> that killed thousands of Algerians; estimates range from a few thousand to tens of thousands. On 1 November 1954 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-liberation-front-greece">National Liberation Front</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-liberation-front-algeria">FLN</a>) launched coordinated attacks across the country. The war escalated after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippeville-massacre">Philippeville massacre</a> of August 1955, and in 1957 paratroopers under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jacques-massu">Jacques Massu</a> broke the FLN network in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-algiers">Battle of Algiers</a> through the systematic use of torture. Settler and army anger at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> produced the crisis of May 1958, which returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> to power; he moved towards accepting self-determination, announced in September 1959, and survived the Generals’ Putsch of April 1961 and the terrorism of the settler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#organisation-armee-secrete">Secret Army Organisation</a> (OAS). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#evian-accords">Évian Accords</a> of 18 March 1962 ended the war, a referendum on 1 July approved independence almost unanimously, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> became independent on 5 July 1962. Historians commonly estimate Algerian deaths at roughly 250,000 to 400,000, against the official Algerian figure of 1.5 million; around 800,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pieds-noirs">pieds-noirs</a> left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, and many harkis who had served France were killed.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>’s great settler colony, governed for 132 years as part of France on a basis of land seizure and legal inequality; its violent conquest and the war of independence that ended it in 1962 reshaped both modern Algeria and the French Republic and became a defining episode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#decolonisation">decolonisation</a>.</em></p>

